# Hybrid field-based KTP OCR

Sprint doc: `docs/sprints/revision-patient-ktp-ocr-field-based-roi-1.md`.
Extends rule 177 (suggestions) and rule 178 (pilot gate); neither is relaxed.
Where this rule and 177 disagree on how a value is READ, this rule wins; on
what may be APPLIED, 177 still governs.

## What the hybrid read is

`resources/js/ktp-roi-ocr.js` (pure, DOM-free) finds the card, straightens it
to a fixed size, places a box per field from a versioned template, and reads
each box with a field-specific engine setup. The whole-card read from the
previous release still runs on the same straightened card. The server
(`KtpOcrSuggestionService`) parses both and reconciles them field by field.
`resources/js/ktp-roi-ui.js` is the operator surface (lazy chunk).

## Foundations

1. **Hybrid, not replacement.** Both the whole-card read and the field-box read
   run. Neither may be removed to save time without a measured benchmark that
   shows the remaining one is at least as safe on wrong pre-filled values.
2. **A card that cannot be found is not guessed.** Boundary detection falls back
   to the full image (`boundary.found = false`) and tells the operator; it never
   invents a crop. The operator may set the four corners by hand.
3. **Coordinates are normalized** (0..1) on the straightened card, never pixels
   of a particular camera. Boxes are always clamped inside the card and keep a
   minimum size (`ROI_LIMITS.minBoxSize`).
4. **The template is versioned** (`KTP_TEMPLATE.version`, now `ektp-v1`). A
   change to box positions is a new version, re-benchmarked, never an edit in
   place. Label anchoring may move a row; it may not invent one.
5. **Boxes are editable** by mouse, touch and keyboard (arrows move,
   Shift+arrows resize), with no page scroll while dragging, and can be reset
   to the auto-anchored position.
6. **Preprocessing is bounded and deterministic**: at most two passes per field
   (enhanced, binarized), cap height normalized; no unbounded retry loop.
7. **Each field has its own character set.** The NIK reads digits only and must
   be exactly 16 digits; it is never padded, completed, inferred from the birth
   date or chosen from a near match. Dates must be real calendar dates. Gender
   is normalized to the canonical values.
8. **Disagreement is surfaced, never resolved by confidence.** When the two
   reads differ, the field is `conflict`: both values are shown, neither is
   pre-selected, and the operator picks or types. Agreement may confirm a
   glyph-corrected value; it never turns an invalid value into a valid one.
   A read that disagreed with itself — the whole-card read seeing a label
   twice, or a field's two image passes differing — cannot be pre-selected on
   its own. A NIK in conflict is still cross-checked, against every candidate.
9. **A retry ("Baca Ulang") never overwrites** a value the operator accepted or
   typed. The new read is offered beside it and needs the operator to take it.
   An untouched row takes the new read UNSELECTED — a tick the system set on an
   earlier read is not an acceptance.
10. **The server owns parsing and labels.** The browser sends text and
    confidence only (`lines`, optional `fields`); the server chooses field
    labels, validates, cross-checks the NIK and builds suggestions. A box read
    is filed only under its own field. A request without `fields` returns the
    previous response shape (values differ only where a misread RT/RW label is
    now recognised).
11. **The parse endpoint still stores, logs and audits nothing** and performs no
    patient lookup (rule 177 §7). Bounded payload: known field keys only,
    bounded text length, confidence 0..100.
12. **Only existing `mst_patients` columns are applied.** Birth place, religion
    and marital status stay display-only. No new column or migration without an
    explicit schema decision.
13. **Every existing control stays**: authentication, RBAC, branch isolation,
    the pilot gate (rule 178), the feature flag, private storage, NIK masking,
    CSRF, the `ktp-scan` rate limit, the explicit `ktp_ocr_verified`
    confirmation and the submit-time duplicate check.
14. **KTP images never leave the browser for OCR.** Self-hosted tesseract.js
    only; no external OCR, AI or MCP service, no CDN fallback.
15. **No KTP data in logs, telemetry, test fixtures, benchmarks or charts.** The
    benchmark corpus is fictional and machine-made
    (`tools/ktp-ocr-benchmark/generate_synthetic_ktp.py`); reports carry counts
    and timings only.
16. **OCR text is untrusted.** DOM writes use `.value` / `.textContent` only;
    the UI makes exactly one request, to the parse URL the page hands it.
17. **Resources are bounded**: source pixels capped from the image header
    before decoding, one worker per page, worker terminated after idle, crops
    released after use. A session can be closed at any point: every step is
    raced against its lifetime, so closing never leaves the page waiting.
18. **Confident is not correct** (rule 177 §4). Do not auto-apply, do not remove
    the operator check, do not rank alternatives by confidence.
19. **Synthetic results are not real-card accuracy.** Real-KTP accuracy is
    `PENDING` until the supervised pilot (runbook §3–§6) runs with recorded
    consent (decision D7). A code release never widens the pilot scope or arms
    the flag; `PATIENT_KTP_OCR_PILOT_*` keys change only by owner decision.

## Measured on the synthetic corpus (48 cards × 6 fields = 288 reads)

Whole-card read (previous release): 216 correct, 39 wrong values pre-filled.
Hybrid: 237 correct, 49 left for the operator to choose, 2 wrong pre-filled;
pre-filled precision 99.1%. Node median read time 1.72 s (was 0.90 s).
These numbers are fixtures for regression, not a claim about real cards.

## Gotchas

- `recognize(image, options)` options are per call and restored by
  tesseract.js; pass `null` for the whole-card read so field whitelists never
  leak into it.
- An unrecognised label line (e.g. `RTIRW`) loses a whole field in the
  whole-card read; the field-box read recovers it because the server attaches
  the label itself.
- A photo-caption column bleeds into the occupation and address lines of a
  whole-card read; that is the main reason the field boxes exist.
